Vercel Speed Insights Installation Complete

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the Next.js application following the latest official documentation.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.0.0
- Updated package.json to include the new dependency
- Updated package-lock.json with the new package and its dependencies

### 2. Integration into Layout
Modified: `app/layout.tsx`
- Added import statement: `import { SpeedInsights } from '@vercel/speed-insights/next'`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component at the end of the body element
- Placement follows Vercel's recommended practice for Next.js App Router (v13.5+)

## Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel Speed Insights documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart), I implemented the direct import approach suitable for Next.js v13.5+. Since this project uses Next.js 16.2.12, this is the recommended method.

The SpeedInsights component was placed:
- Inside the `<body>` tag of the root layout
- After all other content and components
- This ensures it doesn't interfere with the application's rendering while still collecting performance metrics

## Verification

✅ Build completed successfully with `npm run build`
✅ No linting errors introduced in modified files
✅ Package dependencies properly installed and locked
✅ TypeScript compilation successful

## What This Enables

Once deployed to Vercel, Speed Insights will:
- Automatically collect real user performance metrics
- Track Core Web Vitals (LCP, FID, CLS, FCP, TTFB, INP)
- Provide performance analytics in the Vercel dashboard
- Help identify performance bottlenecks in production

No additional configuration is required. The integration will automatically start collecting data after the next deployment to Vercel.
